#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148392} :activity/id #uuid "0d7d228b-78f0-507d-898b-cc2078977de4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148392 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148771 :tutorial/content "There are three types of equilibrium solutions for an *autonomous* differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = f(y),\n$$\n\nwhere the right-hand side doesn't depend explicitly on $x.$\nWe can classify each using the differential equation's phase line.\n\nLet's see each type, and classify the equilibrium solutions for the differential equation whose phase line is shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6fc21e9c83b98e055751ebd4062a3019687a885e2561191ec2d0bfd9de36a85.png)\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a **sink** if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\nFor the phase line above, $y=2$ is a sink.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a **source** if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution. \nFor the phase line above, $y=0$ is a source.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a **node** if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\nFor the phase line above, $y=-2$ is a node since the arrows above and below it both point downward." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f2f0bc9-a043-5c8e-92aa-88135e4033a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5572 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e343aa9-f699-5b3e-a382-effdfef6dd15" :step/math-academy-id 594276 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148393}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148393 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147389 :question/id #uuid "bb252f4f-ef82-52bc-a485-f88f0de96173" :question/math-academy-id "e-8650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395535}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"} :step/id #uuid "29698ec0-1dda-5f69-bb67-f92175c1a371" :step/math-academy-id 594277 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147387 :question/id #uuid "7aa0a0a8-05d4-532c-a830-f1df43514fdf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8538"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375611}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "f7615658-7aa8-5de3-95ff-2d74b5889e16" :step/math-academy-id 594280 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148395}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147385 :question/id #uuid "eed50b24-e97c-51c9-b625-3327783b6374" :question/math-academy-id "e-8536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374986}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "060c8c47-3b5f-5a36-b80e-757102d6f065" :step/math-academy-id 594283 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147497 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147386 :question/id #uuid "dba1deed-4311-54fe-a690-8d89d4cb9592" :question/math-academy-id "e-8537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414153}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"} :step/id #uuid "5b0fcd37-0446-59cb-b89e-24c477e9a1cc" :step/math-academy-id 594286}] :activity/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147712}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414157} :activity/id #uuid "8f67b1d8-cc12-5be2-bedd-7d8ea39d1438" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048131 :topic/id #uuid "81e52842-e868-5110-93ce-65b6024b5770" :topic/math-academy-id 2533} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463df15c-5e98-5585-9c0e-3ceb78f63f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467142} {:answer/id #uuid "9c48c703-8eb3-5e1a-bf9b-43c59c2b0628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467142} :answer-field/id #uuid "e974aded-bb59-5eb7-91a9-b986f8275520" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468292} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435fd677-3e77-505c-8238-4922b6ce07b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467144} {:answer/id #uuid "c641b6ca-e405-5c2a-85db-dd0a74418ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467144} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2b04eed-063d-534c-b713-a7b132e0c86d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddeaf159-2715-57f8-9991-d2da5b57dbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467146} {:answer/id #uuid "91d20914-0764-54d3-9d56-9a3b6a00b760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467147} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d08b7a2-aa58-5bad-9650-971b2a861ebb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a963a554-e172-5944-b345-2fca4048d858" :question/math-academy-id "q-339709" :question/problem "![](images/78/78c4fafeaa4015c98d2a1817e47556957e00c87e265ca934c361a5f5941b6419.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=-2$ is a source.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/90/9003cc9588b0838527f018463e2077d2d81bb5930ab70c1e3c713832e207bd99.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "4301353b-f307-5ad7-9fa6-573ee6bd3b91"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a8a7f75-ccd9-5462-8bb7-2c7b1be60406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b5/b5e7a0272bb45b9d83ec8864f58cf3b96cbb6399d079f23d168d8de5416a1c23.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414141} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd318dd-9201-58a9-b18d-379eb2818d97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8d/8d608e5ab231af991d4c55ca863e95557faae55bf44ad0e8e99f7cd0417531f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414142} {:answer/id #uuid "80ba1f07-d31e-5ece-ad1d-7de624148041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3e/3ed74c49c2218d24dd5235f6a2f6df948d14a58edd6872ccef17ea0434c41c2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414146} {:answer/id #uuid "b82bf18a-7b1c-5303-9140-3233520ff812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/9a/9a24e2ecc59eb8d152790c6850403f74fe9193cc9e3dd9f3d6ff6f38e494752d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414148} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6b5c8a-efde-53a2-864f-7808884654c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ae/ae926b04f1a8bb8c22cd72208b6416aa443e864083dbe4d8c2c737c049ead4f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414149}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414141} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c0eb52d-5859-5f17-a2af-047313c84ebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "57d5be79-8453-52cc-b08d-ee25cf4792e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-98812" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-3,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](images/a4/a4d0e0f44f77e0d29d6d226b058806e947b38f4784dc61ea609ac1040b73d7dd.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/b5/b5e7a0272bb45b9d83ec8864f58cf3b96cbb6399d079f23d168d8de5416a1c23.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."} :step/id #uuid "4bcfd21f-7077-56a5-b180-c56af3baefcb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7472dd-4374-53b0-ab00-c75e67df452a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361116} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5472b2-d6d6-52ea-a044-09bb2a1127bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361376} {:answer/id #uuid "858d2406-7a62-530d-b3f3-7ae4483ae919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361434} {:answer/id #uuid "880f5f32-2604-52b9-a6aa-0210178b0d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a source and $y=-3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361442} {:answer/id #uuid "94cbc50d-4636-5057-ad39-889859bff52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y=3$ is a sink and $y=-3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361434} :answer-field/id #uuid "431fc151-9f48-50ea-8f92-91e1f1278462" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9765a7a-f1b5-599a-907d-e5751bd4bc1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98761" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+3y+9$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/24/244a46b17ad69e8d7cc3ae4f3d9c3c46c1d6e6a7ea154b849946f8ece6030071.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"."} :step/id #uuid "4cca875a-cd23-5293-b9eb-53796fc6ca53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f13d25-8aaa-53fe-b649-79f759aca692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460461} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5118f7-bea8-56bb-9fa7-7066d9d15341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460461} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ed60369-6527-596a-aa20-10745731f2e3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461097} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58384368-2e9c-513d-884f-c895ead26452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460463} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab5835d-dd16-5c59-b329-2d76fad3fea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460464} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4a2a63-53b5-5ff5-adec-a8a211a46343" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461098} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56d3d31-42e8-5b71-9337-634db804ceee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460465} {:answer/id #uuid "5632df88-4d15-5fcb-8683-ca20c35ee138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186460465} :answer-field/id #uuid "b652d627-c7ef-5e71-8927-79a18df27daa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "68af5d21-8052-5193-93c5-650cda389ee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-96560" :question/problem "![](images/93/936d303dcc89c5c27e77fa3d7cfc8b63643284d9e203f176c9e0c5588c27e049.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a node.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=3$ is a source.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/69/69e895d4091075f6d50eb9e44dc027c342d1fe7ca0269224c9b6a019ab312c77.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "d28d673b-fc35-5482-abfa-3f1e7616614a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037a7d4f-c46b-5ef5-9f12-e499f543ede1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/31/31519aa878fd6a9b450296552afd9bbf628e30db68a2d7c6dc6b02a48ae28b06.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360845} {:answer/id #uuid "0562a68a-9113-579a-9191-136bc8b91243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a3/a375a537b104f5bcdd8f5951277bce2494d8861a7212772a7d7050fa14091cb9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360849} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9fb8ad-c027-5308-86ad-a2260380cc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d7/d72110682c1959f10546b6fa49d6e4ebedf6b6d5557639c772f28a920f306df5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361050} {:answer/id #uuid "3b5da285-1e73-5eac-bb63-ee605aca7f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/41/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361109} {:answer/id #uuid "bc463e5f-25db-5272-b846-75dffcfba6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c7/c7895d3f3db254736c4e20ff30dbe92fb7c0786291b1198ed3f727971268be31.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361109} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd49cbaa-d57f-5e0e-9cec-db71bf257efe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2a6368a-0d2f-5f5f-a7f7-7f9694dc9333" :question/math-academy-id "q-98774" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](images/e5/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/41/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line."} :step/id #uuid "dc1c1dd6-77b7-596c-aadc-9bd547e58f0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414155}}] :activity/title "Classifying Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414140}]}
